   "body": "## A wedding, an uncle, a question\n\nPicture it. You're ten, maybe eleven. There's a wedding or a family lunch, and an uncle leans in, smiling, and asks: *What do you want to be when you grow up?*\n\nIt's a kind question. He means well. And you already know which answers will make the adults nod.\n\n## The answers were always the same\n\nDoctor. Engineer. Teacher. CA. IAS. These days, maybe… YouTuber.\n\nIt was a short list, and you heard it so often that it started to feel like the whole world. Even YouTuber, the one that sounds rebellious, is still on the same tiny menu. It's the \"safe rebel\" choice. It feels brave, but it still comes from the same handful of options everyone has already heard.\n\nRead that list slowly and something becomes obvious. **That is not a choice. That is a cage with six windows.**\n\nThat line is meant to sting a little, but it needs a second half straight away. The people who gave you that menu love you. They offered the jobs they knew, the ones they had seen work out for someone. That's care. The menu is still too small.\n\n> A menu is not the same thing as a world.\n\n## What CAM is, in one line\n\nCAM stands for Career Advisory Movement. It began as a keynote for Classes 8 to 12 called **Exposure Is All You Need**, and the talk makes one promise early on:\n\n**Not a career. A wider map.**\n\nWe won't pick your life for you. We'll show you that it's bigger than the menu. If you came here hoping someone would just tell you what to take in Class 11, this is the honest answer: nobody should decide that for you from a list of six. You deserve to see more first.\n\n## Try this before you read on\n\nThe talk asks students to do something simple, and it works just as well on your own:\n\n**Name every career you know.** Sixty seconds. No phone. Be honest and write down only what you actually know.\n\nThen look at your list. How many are outside doctor, engineer and CA? In most rooms the pile stays small. Plenty of students land somewhere around twelve, and they're mostly the same twelve.\n\n> Familiar is not the same as plenty.\n\nThat isn't a failing. You weren't lazy and you aren't uncurious. Nobody ever put the other names in front of you. The next article is about that gap, and it has a name.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\n- Read **The Exposure Gap** to see why most students only know 10–20 careers.\n- Start **Day 1 of Thirty Days of Looking**: write every career you can name in five minutes, then keep the list.\n- Keep that uncle's question in mind. By the end of this series you'll have a much longer answer, and it'll be yours.",
