   "body": "## The real question\n\nAsk a room of students why most of them know only ten or twenty careers, and you'll hear the usual answers: studies, parents, movies. They're all partly true. But underneath them is one word: **exposure**.\n\n> You can only choose from what you have seen. Not from what exists.\n\nThat's the whole argument in one sentence. It's worth reading twice.\n\n## Naming the problem\n\nCAM calls it **the Exposure Gap**: *the distance between careers that exist and careers you have been shown.*\n\nPicture a canyon. On the left cliff is everything that exists, all the work people actually do. On the right cliff is what a Class 10 student has actually met. Your future gets decided somewhere in the air between them.\n\n## You cannot want a life you have never met\n\nHere's an example from the talk. A child who has never met a type designer can't \"have a passion\" for type. Passion isn't some feeling that's already in you waiting to be found. Very often it's **first contact plus time**.\n\nSo when a student says \"I don't know my passion,\" that's not a confession. It's a normal result of having only been shown a brochure.\n\n## Exposure is uneven\n\nThe gap doesn't look the same for everyone.\n\n**A city student** sees offices, metros, studios, start-ups and tuitions. By Class 12 they might know two hundred job names. That's still a fraction.\n\n**A town student** sees what the town can see: teacher, doctor, shop, army, maybe a relative working in the Gulf. That's still a fraction too.\n\nNeither of them is worse off as a person, and neither group needs to feel ashamed. The point is simpler. **Neither has seen two thousand.** Privilege changes the size of the window. It doesn't change the size of the sky.\n\n## A hard swap\n\nThis idea frees a lot of students, so it's worth slowing down for:\n\n**Interest is not a personality trait. Interest is a side-effect of exposure.**\n\nIf you're waiting to feel a calling before you start looking, you've got the order backwards. You look first. Then interest has something to grow on.\n\n## What to do with this\n\n- Go back to your sixty-second list. Count how many jobs are outside the usual six. That number is your gap, measured.\n- Don't treat the gap as a verdict on you. It's a sign of what you haven't been shown yet.\n- Read **There Are Not Twelve Careers** next. That's where the map gets wider.",
