   "body": "## You didn't love cricket before you saw cricket\n\nSomeone put a bat in your hands, or a broadcast on the TV, or a maidan in front of you. Then the hours did the rest.\n\nSwap cricket for dance, coding, cooking or animals and it works the same way: **exposure, then time, then identity.**\n\n## The chain\n\n**1. Exposure: you meet the thing.**\nA name, a video, a person, a tool. Something you didn't know about is suddenly real.\n\n**2. Curiosity: you lean closer.**\nYou watch one more video. You ask one more question. You notice it out in the world.\n\n**3. Practice: hours turn into skill.**\nThis part is slow and not very glamorous. It's where most of the real change happens.\n\n**4. Identity: people start calling you \"the one who…\"**\nThe one who draws. The one who fixes phones. The one who knows every stat.\n\nLook at where identity sits. It comes **last**. Waiting to \"know who I am\" before you start exploring gets the chain backwards. You can't skip to step four.\n\n## The career you \"always wanted\"\n\nThere's a quiet truth in the talk that's worth sitting with:\n\n> The career you \"always wanted\" is often just the career you saw first.\n\nThat doesn't mean your early dream is wrong. It might be exactly right. It does mean it's worth checking whether you chose it or simply met it before anything else.\n\n## Four patterns, not four celebrities\n\nCAM shares four composite paths. They're archetypes, labelled honestly as composites rather than real named people. The pattern is real even when nobody is famous. As you read, look for the first exposure in each one.\n\n- **Ananya, Pune.** She loved arranging her cupboard more than she cared about marks. Now she's a wayfinding designer for a metro. She still arranges things, only now it's stations instead of shelves.\n- **Kabir, Kochi.** He failed a medical entrance and kept borrowing his uncle's camera. Now he shoots underwater films for a marine lab. The ocean became the patient.\n- **Meera, Shillong.** She was told \"music is a hobby\". Now she designs sound for electric cars, and every door-thunk is a composition.\n- **Dev, a small town in Rajasthan.** He scored well, and his family wanted CA. He liked cricket stats more than ledgers. Now he's a sports data analyst for a franchise. It's still spreadsheets, just a different game.\n\nThe first exposures were a cupboard, a camera, music and cricket stats. Each story follows the same pattern: first contact, then hours of practice, then a door opening, often with one stubborn adult somewhere along the way.\n\n## Try it\n\nWrite down one thing you lose track of time doing. Then ask: where did I first meet it? That's your chain, already in motion.",
