   "body": "## How to play\n\nEach clue describes a real working day, or a \"real Tuesday\", in the first person. Read it, guess the job, then read the answer. Wrong answers aren't failures. **They're the Exposure Gap, caught in the act.**\n\nTo make it harder, cover the answers and choose from the four options.\n\n---\n\n### 1. \"I walk forests at 4am with a camera that looks like a tree stump. I am not shooting a wedding.\"\n*Hint: the tiger never poses.*\nOptions: Wedding Photographer · Forest Guard · Camera-trap Technician · Nature Vlogger\n**Answer: Camera-trap Technician.** They hide cameras, change batteries, and wait. Wildlife films and science both hire them.\n\n### 2. \"I make the footsteps you hear in a film using celery, shoes and a bunch of keys. The actor is already home.\"\n*Hint: sound, not picture.*\nOptions: Stunt Double · Foley Artist · Choreographer · Prop Master\n**Answer: Foley Artist.** Named after Jack Foley. Every rustle in a movie is often built later, by hand.\n\n### 3. \"I count a company's invisible exhaust the way a CA counts money. My spreadsheet is made of carbon.\"\n*Hint: climate, not accounts class.*\nOptions: Chartered Accountant · Carbon Accountant · Tax Lawyer · Bank Teller\n**Answer: Carbon Accountant.** Companies now need people who can measure emissions honestly, not just print a green poster.\n\n### 4. \"I design the signs in a metro so you find Platform 6 without asking a stranger. If you notice my work, I have failed.\"\n*Hint: cities, not art class.*\nOptions: Graphic Designer · Architect · Wayfinding Designer · Traffic Police\n**Answer: Wayfinding Designer.** Airports, hospitals and campuses hire people whose job is making sure nobody gets lost.\n\n### 5. \"I write letters that millions will read and never notice. A newspaper hired me for the shape of 'क' and 'R'.\"\n*Hint: not a novelist.*\nOptions: Calligraphy Teacher · Type Designer · Printer · English Lecturer\n**Answer: Type Designer.** Every app, board exam paper and shop sign uses type someone drew. India needs designers for many scripts.\n\n### 6. \"I fly a camera where a crane cannot go. Sometimes I map a farm. Sometimes I film a storm.\"\n*Hint: a pilot, but the passenger is a lens.*\nOptions: Pilot · Drone Cinematographer · Meteorologist · Surveyor\n**Answer: Drone Cinematographer.** Film sets, farms, mines and disaster sites all need drone pilots with a good eye, and the best ones are booked years ahead.\n\n### 7. \"I grow a cricket pitch like a living instrument. Grass height can change a Test match.\"\n*Hint: the ground staff's scientist.*\nOptions: Gardener · Pitch Curator · Umpire · Groundsman\n**Answer: Pitch Curator.** Curators blend soil science, weather and cricket law. Stadiums pay well for this quiet job.\n\n### 8. \"I sit with a family while a blood-test becomes a life decision. I do not prescribe medicines.\"\n*Hint: genes, not general practice.*\nOptions: Doctor · Lab Technician · Genetic Counsellor · Pharmacist\n**Answer: Genetic Counsellor.** As tests get cheaper, someone has to help people understand what a result means, and what it doesn't.\n\n### 9. \"I write the personality of a machine that will talk to millions. If it sounds rude, that is on me.\"\n*Hint: words, not neural networks.*\nOptions: Coder · Call Centre Agent · Conversation Designer · English Teacher\n**Answer: Conversation Designer.** Banks, health helplines and farming advisories need people who design how AI talks, and when it should stop.\n\n### 10. \"I garden underwater. My plants are coral. My season is a spawning night.\"\n*Hint: the reef is the farm.*\nOptions: Scuba Instructor · Marine Biologist · Coral Restoration Diver · Fisherman\n**Answer: Coral Restoration Diver.** Labs and coastal groups now hire divers who know biology, not just how to take scuba selfies.\n\n---\n\n## What the game shows\n\nCount how many you got right. Most people get some right without ever having heard the job title before. You could tell what the work was. You just didn't know what it was called.\n\n**You already have instincts. You needed names.** Learning the names is a kind of exposure too.\n\nPlay it with a friend tonight. Swap one clue about a job neither of you has met yet.",
