   "body": "## A relief first\n\n**You do not need to decide your life this week.** You need thirty days of looking.\n\nIf Class 11 subject panic is in the air at your school, read that sentence twice. Nobody's asking for a verdict yet. The ask is much smaller: look properly, a little each day, for a month.\n\nThe CAM site lets you tick a day only when you've actually done it. The rule is simple: **looking counts. Deciding your whole life doesn't.** Nobody else can do this homework for you. Parents can't do it for you either, though they're welcome to join in.\n\nMost days take 10 to 20 minutes. Keep a notebook or a notes app for this month only.\n\n## Days 1–14\n\n### Day 1: Empty the cupboard\nWrite every career you can name in five minutes. No phone. No cousin. No Google.\n*About 5 minutes.*\n\n### Day 2: The real Tuesday\nAsk three adults: 'What did you actually do at work last Tuesday?' Write the verbs, not the job title.\n*About 20 minutes.*\n\n### Day 3: Ten strange names\nOpen the CAM atlas. Pick ten jobs you have never heard. Say them out loud.\n*About 15 minutes.*\n\n### Day 4: One industry, deep\nChoose one industry that is not doctor/engineer. List eight roles inside it.\n*About 15 minutes.*\n\n### Day 5: Follow a tool\nPick an object in your room. Who designed, made, tested, shipped, repaired it? Name those jobs.\n*About 15 minutes.*\n\n### Day 6: Shadow on YouTube\nWatch one 'day in the life' of an unconventional job. Note three things that surprised you.\n*About 20 minutes.*\n\n### Day 7: Map your week\nCircle what already energises you: making, helping, analysing, performing, exploring, organising.\n*About 10 minutes.*\n\n### Day 8: Skill, not subject\nList five things you are already good at that are not exam subjects (explaining, drawing, fixing, noticing).\n*About 10 minutes.*\n\n### Day 9: Lifestyle honesty\nDo you want a desk, a field, a stage, a lab, travel, or home? Rank them 1–6.\n*About 10 minutes.*\n\n### Day 10: Money without myth\nPick two unusual jobs. Find one sentence about how people get paid in that field. Not a salary rumour — a sentence.\n*About 20 minutes.*\n\n### Day 11: Purpose test\nPlanet, people, beauty, truth, play, health — which two would make a bad week still feel worth it?\n*About 10 minutes.*\n\n### Day 12: Talk to a stranger's job\nMessage or ask someone with a job not on your Day 1 list. One question: 'How did you first hear this existed?'\n*About 20 minutes.*\n\n### Day 13: School vs world\nWrite three careers your school talks about. Write seven it never mentions. Hang the list.\n*About 10 minutes.*\n\n### Day 14: Half-time\nRevisit Day 1. Add new names. Count the gap. That gap is the point.\n*About 10 minutes.*\n\n## What the first half is for\n\nDays 1 and 14 are a pair. On Day 1 you empty the cupboard: every career you can name, with no help. On Day 14 you go back to that list, add every new name you've met, and count the difference.\n\n**That gap is the point.** The difference between the two lists is exposure, and you can measure it in a fortnight.\n\nNext: **Thirty days of looking, part 2**, where looking turns into small experiments.",
