   "body": "## From noticing to trying\n\nThe first fourteen days widen the list. The next sixteen test it against real life. You'll make things, walk around your own neighbourhood, explain jobs to younger kids and to your parents, and finally keep two careers while dropping the rest.\n\nSame rule as before: tick a day only when you've actually done it.\n\n## Days 15–30\n\n### Day 15: Future headline\nRead one article about AI, climate, biotech, robots or space. Circle jobs hiding in the paragraph.\n*About 15 minutes.*\n\n### Day 16: Make a tiny thing\nSpend 30 minutes making: a map, a comic, a playlist, a small repair, a recipe. Notice if time disappeared.\n*About 30 minutes.*\n\n### Day 17: The no-list\nWrite three popular careers you do not want — and the honest reason. Permission to not want them.\n*About 10 minutes.*\n\n### Day 18: Local atlas\nWalk 20 minutes from home. Note ten jobs happening around you that are not in textbooks.\n*About 25 minutes.*\n\n### Day 19: Skills you can grow\nPick one flagship job. What skill could you practice this month for free?\n*About 15 minutes.*\n\n### Day 20: Portfolio seed\nStart a folder: photos, writing, code, stats, sketches. Call it 'evidence', not 'hobby'.\n*About 20 minutes.*\n\n### Day 21: Explain it to class 8\nTake one unusual job and explain it in five sentences a younger student would get.\n*About 15 minutes.*\n\n### Day 22: Market, not hype\nIs your favourite job growing, stable, or niche? All three can be good. Just know which.\n*About 15 minutes.*\n\n### Day 23: A day you would hate\nWrite the Tuesday you would dread (night shifts, sales calls, blood, solitude). Let that veto loudly.\n*About 10 minutes.*\n\n### Day 24: Six-lens pass\nRun Passion + Skill + Market + Lifestyle + Purpose + Growth on two careers. Keep the one that scores quieter but truer.\n*About 20 minutes.*\n\n### Day 25: Find a door, not a destiny\nFor one job, find one next step: a club, a short course, a person, a book, a volunteer day.\n*About 20 minutes.*\n\n### Day 26: Family translation\nExplain one unusual career to a parent in their language of safety: training, money path, backup, dignity.\n*About 20 minutes.*\n\n### Day 27: Try a micro-shift\nSpend one hour in the direction of a job: draw type, log cricket data, record street sound, map a park.\n*About 60 minutes.*\n\n### Day 28: Drop two, keep two\nFrom everything this month, keep two careers to explore more. Drop the rest without guilt.\n*About 15 minutes.*\n\n### Day 29: Write future-you\nA one-page letter: what you saw, what surprised you, what you will try next term. Date it.\n*About 20 minutes.*\n\n### Day 30: Don't decide your life\nClose with this sentence: I don't need a final career today. I need a wider map. Then pick Day 1 of the next month.\n*About 10 minutes.*\n\n## How it ends\n\nDay 30 doesn't end with a decision, and that's deliberate. It ends with one sentence:\n\n> I don't need a final career today. I need a wider map.\n\nThen you start Day 1 of next month's looking. The map keeps growing, and so do you.\n\n## For parents and teachers\n\nDay 26, Family translation, is the one to watch for. A student will try to explain an unusual career in the language that matters to you: training, the money path, a backup plan, dignity. Ask real questions and listen to the answers. That conversation is part of the exploring too.",
